### How does a fallback make things worse?

A fallback is intended to preserve availability when the primary path fails. But it runs exactly when the system is already under stress, so a badly designed fallback converts a small failure into a larger one.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FallbackDanger {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Normal: Redis serves 90% of balance reads");
        System.out.println("Redis goes DOWN");
        System.out.println("Fallback: ALL 100% of reads hit PostgreSQL");
        System.out.println("PostgreSQL was sized for 10% miss rate");
        System.out.println("Result: 10x overload --> PostgreSQL dies --> TOTAL outage");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Normal: Redis serves 90% of balance reads
Redis goes DOWN
Fallback: ALL 100% of reads hit PostgreSQL
PostgreSQL was sized for 10% miss rate
Result: 10x overload --> PostgreSQL dies --> TOTAL outage
```

### What is the silent load transfer danger?

When Redis serves 90% of balance reads, PostgreSQL is sized for the 10% miss rate. When Redis fails, the fallback redirects all traffic to PostgreSQL. The database receives 10 times its expected load and collapses.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SilentLoadTransfer {

    // WRONG: unbound fallback transfers full load
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        try {
            return getFromRedis(accountId); // Redis down!
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return getFromDb(accountId); // ALL traffic now hits DB
            // DB was sized for 10% miss rate --> 10x overload --> DB dies
        }
    }

    private BigDecimal getFromRedis(String id) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Redis unavailable");
    }
    private BigDecimal getFromDb(String id) {
        return new BigDecimal("150000.00");
    }
}
```

### What is the stale data danger?

Returning a default or zero balance when the primary fails can be worse than an honest error. A debit operation based on a zero balance would deny Rajesh access to his own money.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class StaleDataDanger {

    // WRONG: returning a default balance
    public BigDecimal getBalanceWrong(String accountId) {
        try {
            return getFromRedis(accountId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return BigDecimal.ZERO; // Rajesh told he has no money!
        }
    }

    // CORRECT: for debit operations, fail closed
    public BigDecimal getBalanceForDebit(String accountId) {
        try {
            return getFromRedis(accountId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new ServiceUnavailableException(
                "Cannot verify balance for " + accountId);
            // Honest error is safer than a confident wrong answer
        }
    }

    private BigDecimal getFromRedis(String id) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Redis unavailable");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StaleDataDanger svc = new StaleDataDanger();
        System.out.println("WRONG balance: " + svc.getBalanceWrong("SAV2001"));
        try {
            svc.getBalanceForDebit("SAV2001");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("CORRECT: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

class ServiceUnavailableException extends RuntimeException {
    public ServiceUnavailableException(String msg) { super(msg); }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG balance: 0
CORRECT: Cannot verify balance for SAV2001
```

### What is the observability masking danger?

The fallback catches the exception and returns a success response, so the success-rate dashboard stays green. Nobody knows Redis is down until PostgreSQL also collapses.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ObservabilityMasking {

    // WRONG: fallback catches exception, dashboard stays GREEN
    // Nobody knows Redis is down until PostgreSQL also dies

    // CORRECT: emit distinct fallback metric
    public BigDecimal getBalanceWithMetric(String accountId,
                                            MeterRegistry meterRegistry) {
        try {
            return getFromRedis(accountId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            meterRegistry.counter("balance.fallback.activated").increment();
            // Alert fires on this counter --> loud signal
            return getFromDb(accountId);
        }
    }

    private BigDecimal getFromRedis(String id) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Redis unavailable");
    }
    private BigDecimal getFromDb(String id) {
        return new BigDecimal("150000.00");
    }
}
```

### How do you fix each danger?

| Problem | Fix |
|---|---|
| Silent load transfer | Bulkhead plus rate limit on fallback; shed excess |
| Stale data | Mark degraded; fail closed for debit operations |
| Observability masking | Distinct fallback-activation metric plus alert |
| Untested fallback | Game-day: kill Redis under load, verify DB survives |

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead.Bulkhead;
import io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead.BulkheadConfig;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BoundedFallback {

    // CORRECT: bulkhead caps fallback at DB's proven capacity
    private final Bulkhead fallbackBulkhead = Bulkhead.of("db-fallback",
        BulkheadConfig.custom().maxConcurrentCalls(20).build());

    public BigDecimal getBalanceSafe(String accountId) {
        try {
            return getFromRedis(accountId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return Bulkhead.decorateSupplier(fallbackBulkhead,
                () -> getFromDb(accountId)).get();
            // Excess beyond 20 concurrent --> rejected --> shed load
        }
    }

    private BigDecimal getFromRedis(String id) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Redis unavailable");
    }
    private BigDecimal getFromDb(String id) {
        return new BigDecimal("150000.00");
    }
}
```

### Why must you test fallback capacity independently?

The fallback path runs only during failure, so it is never exercised under real load during normal operations. It is the least-tested code that runs at exactly the worst moment.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class GameDayTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Game-day test plan:");
        System.out.println("1. Run load test at production traffic volume");
        System.out.println("2. Kill Redis");
        System.out.println("3. Verify: PostgreSQL survives (bulkhead sheds excess)");
        System.out.println("4. Verify: fallback-activation alert fires");
        System.out.println("5. Verify: degraded answers are marked as degraded");
        System.out.println("6. Verify: debit operations fail closed, not silently wrong");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Game-day test plan:
1. Run load test at production traffic volume
2. Kill Redis
3. Verify: PostgreSQL survives (bulkhead sheds excess)
4. Verify: fallback-activation alert fires
5. Verify: degraded answers are marked as degraded
6. Verify: debit operations fail closed, not silently wrong
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is transferring the full primary load to the fallback without bounding it.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class CommonMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: transferring full load to fallback without bounding
        System.out.println("WRONG: Redis fails --> 100% of reads hit DB");
        System.out.println("DB overload --> total outage (worse than Redis alone)");

        // CORRECT: bulkhead caps fallback at DB's proven capacity
        System.out.println("CORRECT: bulkhead allows max 20 concurrent to DB");
        System.out.println("Excess gets 503 --> but DB survives");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: Redis fails --> 100% of reads hit DB
DB overload --> total outage (worse than Redis alone)
CORRECT: bulkhead allows max 20 concurrent to DB
Excess gets 503 --> but DB survives
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- A fallback you have not load-tested independently is a hope, not a safety net.
- Bound the fallback so it receives only what it can handle; reject the rest.
- An honest error is safer than a confident wrong answer for money operations.
- Fallback-activation is a loud signal, not a silent one; alert immediately.
- The fallback path is the least-tested code at the worst possible moment.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class SeniorInsights {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. Untested fallback = hope, not safety net");
        System.out.println("2. Bound with bulkhead; shed excess as 503");
        System.out.println("3. Honest error > confident wrong answer for money");
        System.out.println("4. Fallback-activation metric = loud alert");
        System.out.println("5. Game-day test: kill primary under load, observe");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
1. Untested fallback = hope, not safety net
2. Bound with bulkhead; shed excess as 503
3. Honest error > confident wrong answer for money
4. Fallback-activation metric = loud alert
5. Game-day test: kill primary under load, observe
```
